{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-339693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3xy+y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1) & =y \\\\ (1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{1-(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+1)} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{3{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & ≈-0.67,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈-0.67.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d0e92deeea4b5101cd2da929cea4146a3b1d248358a43bc4637afd491d673da"} {"question" "q-339693" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339693" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.32"] ["math" "-0.67"] ["math" "-3.55"] ["math" "0.32"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339693" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.67"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y(3x-2),\\,y(1)=4\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(3)≈$ {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot (3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{\\text{new}}(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2) & =y \\\\ (1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2)){y}_{\\text{new}} & =y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{(1-2(3{x}_{\\text{new}}-2))} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+2=3.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y}{5-6{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5-6\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{13} \\\\  & ≈-0.31,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈-0.31.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d0e92deeea4b5101cd2da929cea4146a3b1d248358a43bc4637afd491d673da"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.31"] ["math" "-1.52"] ["math" "0.39"] ["math" "2.51"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339694" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.31"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2x-3y,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2{x}_{\\text{new}}-3{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1-\\frac{1}{2})}{5} \\\\  & =-0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=-0.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{2(y-{x}_{\\text{new}})}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-0.6-1)}{5} \\\\  & =-0.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈-0.60,\\,y(1)≈-0.64.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d0e92deeea4b5101cd2da929cea4146a3b1d248358a43bc4637afd491d673da"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.60"] ["math" "-1.32"] ["math" "0.64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.60"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.64"] ["math" "0.46"] ["math" "1.23"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339715" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.64"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}+y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the **implicit** Euler method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1)$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.$\\,y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,y(1)≈$ {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+{e}^{1/2} \\\\  & ≈3.648\\,721,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y≈3.648\\,721.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =2y+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ≈2\\cdot 3.648\\,721+{e}^{1} \\\\  & ≈10.015\\,724,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, rounded to $2$ decimal places, we conclude that \n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.65,\\,y(1)≈10.02.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4d0e92deeea4b5101cd2da929cea4146a3b1d248358a43bc4637afd491d673da"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.73"] ["math" "2.00"] ["math" "3.65"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.65"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505ad341ad43da8"} {"question" "q-339721" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069978/state.json" "file_sha256" "6d5017afb8ec1f436c3f7424030891fe152f921a61e95e4ea505a